  "text": "Building on earlier work that simplified key aspects of the motion of a rigid body that is symmetric about an axis through its centre of mass, this study looks at the curve traced on a unit sphere centred at the fixed point where the sphere meets that axis. Because the geodesic curvature of that spherical trace is central to the analysis, the first part of the paper develops several intrinsic properties of curves on a sphere. These results are of independent interest and lead to many further questions, such as how the curvature expressed as a function of arc length relates to the curve’s overall shape, what extra conditions beyond periodicity guarantee a closed spherical curve, when a planar or spherical curve approaches a closed form, and what comparison statements hold for pairs of curves whose curvatures satisfy simple equalities or inequalities. The second part applies these spherical‑curve findings to the dynamics of a rotating device, revealing some previously unnoticed facts and offering a novel method for establishing key results."
